=== 6.5 One finite system of jets on a smooth model <one-finite-system-of-jets-on-a-smooth-model>

We now convert the strict Seshadri bound into a single surjective jet-evaluation map on a smooth model. Choose $t$ sufficiently small for both Proposition 3.5, Proposition 6.3, and keep it fixed. The next corollary then supplies a point, a Cartier multiple, and finitely many sections whose jets span the required target. These are the finite data used in reduction to positive characteristic; no family of jet systems over varying $t$ is needed.

#thm([Corollary 6.5.])[
  #emph[Fix sufficiently small $t$ as in Proposition 3.5, Proposition 6.3, and let $p : W arrow.r Y$ be any fixed smooth projective resolution. Continue to denote pullbacks by $L$ and $S_0$. On 
$ Z_W = bb(P) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] cal(O)_W \( S_0 \)_1 xor cal(O)_W \( S_0 \)_2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] arrow.r W times W \, #h(2em) M = L_1 + L_2 + q xi \, $

 there are a smooth torus point $z_(*)$, over the isomorphism opens of $p$ in both slots, and a sufficiently divisible integer $k_0 > 0$ such that $k_0 L$ is Cartier and 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.26)")
 $ H^0 \( Z_W \, cal(O)_(Z_W) \( k_0 M \) \) arrow.r cal(O)_(Z_W) \( k_0 M \) ⊗ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] cal(O)_(Z_W \, z_(*)) \/ frak(m)_(z_(*))^(thin 10 k_0 + 1) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $ <eq:fixed-two-slot-jet-map>]

 is surjective. In particular, finitely many sections can be fixed whose jets span its target.]
] <cor:fixed-two-slot-jets>

#proof[
  Choose a very general smooth torus point $z$ of $Z$ above the isomorphism open of $p$ in both slots and satisfying (6.10), so that $epsilon \( P \; z \) > 10$. If $b : hat(Z) arrow.r Z$ is its blowup and $E_z$ is the exceptional divisor, the Seshadri criterion shows that the following rational divisor is ample:
  $ b^(*) P - 10 E_z . $
  Indeed one may choose a rational number strictly between $10$ and $epsilon \( P \; z \)$, and then use the usual ample interval below the Seshadri threshold. Choose $a > 0$ so that $a P$ and $a \( b^(*) P - 10 E_z \)$ are Cartier. Serre vanishing, applied to the fixed sheaf $cal(O)_(hat(Z)) \( - E_z \)$, gives
  $ H^1 #h(-0.16667em) (hat(Z) \, cal(O)_(hat(Z)) \( k b^(*) P - \( 10 k + 1 \) E_z \)) = 0 $
  for all sufficiently large multiples $k$ of $a$. The standard local calculation for the blowup of a smooth point identifies the pushforward of $cal(O)_(hat(Z)) \( - \( 10 k + 1 \) E_z \)$ with $frak(m)_z^(10 k + 1)$, with vanishing higher direct images. The restriction sequence therefore gives surjectivity onto jets through order $10 k$ at $z$.
  The natural morphism $Z_W arrow.r Z$ is an isomorphism near the lift $z_(*)$ of $z$. Pullback preserves the sections in this jet surjection and identifies their jets. Increase the divisible integer $k$ if necessary so that $k L$ is Cartier, and call it $k_0$. Choosing finitely many inverse images of a basis of the finite-dimensional jet target proves the assertion. ◻
]

== 7 Fixed finite data and the final contradiction <sec:frobenius>

The scalar bound and the two-slot jets will now be tested against one independent theorem. We state all its hypotheses before constructing the remaining data. Its independent proof in [38] uses neither abundance nor logarithmic subadditivity.

#thm([Theorem 7.1.])[
  (Finite-data Frobenius incompatibility).  #emph[Let $W$ be a smooth connected projective complex variety of dimension $n gt.eq 2$. Fix rational Cartier divisors $L \, H$, with $H$ ample, an integral Cartier divisor $S$, an integer $q > 0$, and real numbers $r > 1$, $epsilon.alt > 0$. Suppose 
$ H^n lt.eq \( 2 epsilon.alt \)^n \, #h(2em) K_W H^(n - 1) lt.eq 2 H^n \/ r \, #h(2em) \( 2 L + q S \) H^(n - 1) lt.eq 4 H^n \,\
\( 14 epsilon.alt \)^n < 1 \/ 4 \, #h(2em) 80 epsilon.alt < 3 \/ 4 . $

 The following three kinds of fixed data cannot all exist.]
  + #emph[A smooth integral complete-intersection flag ending in a curve $C$, of successive divisor classes $l_i H$ for positive integers $l_i$, with $mu_min \( Omega_W^1 \|_C \) gt.eq 0$.]
  + #emph[On $Z_W = bb(P) \( cal(O) \( S \)_1 xor cal(O) \( S \)_2 \) arrow.r W times W$, in the symmetric-power convention, put $M = L_1 + L_2 + q xi$. For some integer $k_0 > 0$ with $k_0 L$ Cartier, finitely many sections of $k_0 M$ surject onto $cal(O)_(Z_W \, z_(*)) \/ frak(m)_(z_(*))^(\( 2 n + 2 \) k_0 + 1)$ after trivialization at a smooth torus point $z_(*)$.]
  + #emph[A point $x in W$, the blowup $pi_x : hat(W) arrow.r W$ with exceptional divisor $J_x$, and a curve class $gamma = f_(*) \( A_1 dots.h.c A_(n - 1) \)$, where $f : V arrow.r hat(W)$ is a fixed birational morphism from a smooth integral projective variety and the $A_i$ are ample Cartier divisors, such that $J_x gamma > 0$ and 
$ pi_x^(*) \( L + K_W \/ 2 + lambda S \) gamma lt.eq 40 epsilon.alt J_x gamma #h(2em) \( 0 lt.eq lambda lt.eq q \) . $

 It suffices to check the two endpoint inequalities.]
  #emph[No nefness of $L \, S \, K_W$ is assumed, and $z_(*)$ need not lie over $x$. All divisors, the flag, the point, the curve representative, and the finite jet sections are fixed over $bb(C)$ before the residual characteristic varies.]
] <thm:finite-data>

The proof is [38] Theorem 9.1. We shall apply it with $n = 4$, $S = S_0 = iota K$, and $r = r_t$. The remaining work is to obtain its ample perturbation, flag, and actual movable curve witness from the fourfold geometry.

=== 7.1 Fixing the polarization, flag, and movable test <fixing-the-polarization-flag-and-movable-test>

Choose a positive rational number $epsilon.alt$ sufficiently small that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.1)")
 $ \( 14 epsilon.alt \)^n < 1 / 4 \, #h(2em) 80 epsilon.alt < 3 / 4 . $ <eq:epsilon-choice>]

Choose $q$ as in 15, then a sufficiently small rational $t > 0$ so that both jet estimates hold, $r_t > q iota + 1$, and $L^n < \( 2 epsilon.alt \)^n$. Fix $t$ and $r_t$. Let $W arrow.r Y$ be a projective resolution, and continue to denote pullbacks by $K \, A \, L$. Terminality gives

$ K_W = K + E \, #h(2em) E gt.eq 0 upright(" exceptional") \, #h(2em) S_0 = iota K upright(" Cartier") . $

Since $L$ is pulled back from an ample divisor on $Y$,

$ K_W L^(n - 1) = K L^(n - 1) lt.eq L^n \/ r_t . $

Perturb $L$ by a sufficiently small ample rational class on $W$. By continuity, the resulting ample rational divisor $H$ satisfies

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.2)")
 $ H^n lt.eq \( 2 epsilon.alt \)^n \, #h(2em) K_W H^(n - 1) lt.eq frac(2 H^n, r_t) \, #h(2em) \( 2 L + q S_0 \) H^(n - 1) lt.eq 4 H^n . $ <eq:fixed-H>]

For the last inequality, at $H = L$ use $S_0 L^(n - 1) lt.eq iota L^n \/ r_t$ and $q iota < r_t$. We now fix $H$; all further geometric data will be chosen once over $bb(C)$ before reduction.

The divisor $K_W$ is pseudo-effective. Generic semipositivity, in the smooth empty-boundary case of [6] Theorem 2.1, gives nonnegative $H^(n - 1)$-slope to every positive-rank torsion-free quotient of $Omega_W^1$. Apply the Mehta–Ramanathan restriction theorem to the Harder–Narasimhan filtration and its factors [33] Theorem 6.1 and Remark 6.2. We obtain a fixed flag of smooth integral general very ample sections, of types

$ h_i = l_i H quad \( 1 lt.eq i lt.eq n - 1 \) \, $

ending in a smooth curve $C$, such that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.3)")
 $ mu_min \( Omega_W^1 \|_C \) gt.eq 0 . $ <eq:fixed-curve-slope>]

The $l_i$ are sufficiently large divisible integers, chosen successively. The final general curve avoids the codimension-two loci where the filtration factors fail to be locally free. Set $Lambda = product_(i = 1)^(n - 1) l_i$. Then

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.4)")
 $ \[ C \] = Lambda H^(n - 1) \, #h(2em) h_i dot.op C = l_i Lambda H^n . $ <eq:flag-degrees>]

Next choose a very general point $x in W$ above the isomorphism locus of $W arrow.r Y$, and let $pi_x : hat(W) arrow.r W$ be its blowup, with exceptional divisor $J_x$. Put

$ D^(+) = 2 r_t \( K + 2 t A \) + 2 E . $

By 9, every nonzero section of a sufficiently divisible multiple $k D^(+)$ satisfies

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.5)")
 $ "ord"_x \( s \) lt.eq 32 k epsilon.alt . $ <eq:Dplus-order>]

Indeed, pushing down removes only the effective exceptional part, and is an isomorphism near $x$.

#thm([Lemma 7.2.])[
  #emph[There is a strongly movable curve class $gamma$ on $hat(W)$, represented as the pushforward of an ample complete intersection on a fixed smooth projective birational model, such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.6)")
 $ \( pi_x^(*) D^(+) \) dot.op gamma < 40 epsilon.alt thin J_x dot.op gamma \, #h(2em) J_x dot.op gamma > 0 . $ <eq:movable-test>]

]
] <lem:fixed-movable-test>

#proof[
  If $pi_x^(*) D^(+) - 40 epsilon.alt J_x$ were pseudo-effective, then for any rational $c$ with $32 epsilon.alt < c < 40 epsilon.alt$ the divisor $pi_x^(*) D^(+) - c J_x$ would be big: it is a positive convex combination of the preceding pseudo-effective divisor and the big divisor $pi_x^(*) D^(+)$. A sufficiently divisible effective multiple would contradict (7.5). Thus $pi_x^(*) D^(+) - 40 epsilon.alt J_x$ is not pseudo-effective.
  Movable-curve duality [5] Theorem 2.2 gives a strongly movable class with negative pairing against that divisor. Since the inequality is strict, we may take an actual pushforward of an ample complete intersection on one smooth birational projective model, approximating the ample classes rationally and clearing their denominators if needed. The big divisor $pi_x^(*) D^(+)$ has positive pairing with this nonzero class: write it as an ample class plus an effective class and pull back to the chosen model. The strict inequality therefore implies $J_x dot.op gamma > 0$. ◻
]

We omit $pi_x^(*)$ in subsequent intersection formulas involving $gamma$. The concrete complete-intersection representative will continue to test effective divisors after reduction.

=== 7.2 Endpoint comparison <endpoint-comparison>

For $0 lt.eq lambda lt.eq q$, put $Q_lambda = L + K_W \/ 2 + lambda S_0$. The actual divisor classes on the fixed resolution satisfy

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.7)")
 $ D^(+) - Q_lambda = \( r_t - 1 \/ 2 - lambda iota \) K + 3 r_t t A + 3 / 2 E . $ <eq:slot-domination>]

The first coefficient is positive because $r_t > q iota + 1$. The pullbacks of $K \, A$ are nef, and $E$ is effective. A strongly movable curve pairs nonnegatively with each of these classes. Consequently 19 gives

$ Q_lambda gamma lt.eq D^(+) gamma < 40 epsilon.alt J_x gamma #h(2em) \( 0 lt.eq lambda lt.eq q \) . $

This verifies the curve inequalities in 18. It does not require a pseudo-effective cone to specialize to positive characteristic: the curve has the fixed ample complete-intersection representative required by that theorem.

Take $z_(*) \, k_0$, and the finite sections supplied by 17 on this resolution. For clarity about the finite choices in the comparison, choose also an integral ample divisor $T_0$ so that each $T_0 + a S_0$, for $0 lt.eq a lt.eq \( k_0 - 1 \) q$, has fixed sections nonvanishing at the two base coordinates of $z_(*)$. Serre generation permits these finitely many choices. They supply the residual degrees when the common proof puts $B_p = k_0 floor.l p \/ k_0 floor.r L + T_0$. The differences $B_p - p L$ belong to a fixed finite list.

We have now verified all the hypotheses of 18. The choices were made in the order $n = 4 \, epsilon.alt$; then the gap $delta$ and integer $q$; then $t \, r_t$; then the resolution, $H$, flag, point, and curve; and finally the finite jets and fillers. Only after these choices does its proof let $p$ tend to infinity.

=== 7.3 The two orders of the determinant <the-two-orders-of-the-determinant>

We recall the comparison in the proof of [38] Theorem 9.1, identifying the determinant bundles to which our movable test applies. Spread the fixed data as in that proof and work on a smooth reduction in sufficiently large characteristic $p$. The same notation denotes the reduced divisors and witnesses. For relative Frobenius $F : W arrow.r W'$, let $S'_0$ be the base-field twist of $S_0$ and put

$ cal(E) = F_(*) cal(O)_W \( B_p \) \, #h(2em) s = "rk" cal(E) = p^n \, #h(2em) R = s^2 = p^8 \, #h(2em) U_a = H^0 \( W \, cal(O)_W \( B_p + p a S_0 \) \) . $

The projection formula gives an evaluation map

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.8)")
 $ Phi_p : xor.big_(a + b = q\
a \, b gt.eq 0) U_a ⊗ U_b ⊗ cal(O)_(W' times W') \( - a S'_(0 \, 1) - b S'_(0 \, 2) \) arrow.r cal(E) times.square cal(E) . $ <eq:finite-evaluation>]

Products of the fixed jet sections, multiplied by the fixed fillers, generate the local quotient by the $p$-th powers of the parameters at $z_(*)$. Let $A_(*)$ be the tensor product of the two base Frobenius-fiber algebras. If the torus coordinate $z$ has value $z_0 eq.not 0$ at $z_(*)$, this quotient is $A_(*) \[ z \] \/ \( z^p - z_0^p \)$. Project to the coefficient of $1$ in its $A_(*)$-basis $1 \, z \, dots.h \, z^(p - 1)$. Exactly the weights divisible by $p$ survive, showing that the values of (7.8) span $A_(*)$. Its dimension is $R$, so $Phi_p$ has generic rank $R$.

On the diagonal, the columns instead restrict to global sections of one line bundle on the Frobenius fiber product:

$ Delta_F = W times_(W') W \, #h(2em) cal(L)_p = cal(O)_(Delta_F) \( B_(p \, 1) + B_(p \, 2) + p q S_(0 \, 1) \) . $

Here $p S_(0 \, 1)$ and $p S_(0 \, 2)$ are isomorphic line bundles on $Delta_F$, so all the weight pairs $a + b = q$ give this same bundle. The rank at every diagonal point is at most $h^0 \( Delta_F \, cal(L)_p \)$. The diagonal-ideal filtration has graded bundles

$ cal(G)_j = cal(O)_W \( 2 B_p + p q S_0 \) ⊗ A_j \( Omega_W^1 \) \, #h(2em) 0 lt.eq j lt.eq n \( p - 1 \) \, $

where $A_j$ is the degree-$j$ part of the symmetric algebra modulo the $p$-th powers of its local generators. If $e_j = "rk" A_j$, then $sum_j e_j = p^n$. The slope estimate and our one fixed flag give the common bound proved in the companion:

$ h^0 \( W \, cal(G)_j \) lt.eq e_j p^n #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 7^n H^n + O \( p^(- 1) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

The error is independent of $j$. Summing therefore bounds every diagonal rank by $R \( 7^n H^n + O \( p^(- 1) \) \) < R \/ 4$, since $7^n H^n lt.eq \( 14 epsilon.alt \)^n < 1 \/ 4$.

Choose $R$ generically independent columns of $Phi_p$, and let $m_i$ be the sum of their weights in slot $i$. Their nonzero determinant is a section

$ 0 eq.not sigma in H^0 \( W' times W' \, cal(Q)_1 times.square cal(Q)_2 \) \, #h(2em) cal(Q)_i = \( det cal(E) \)^(⊗ s) ⊗ cal(O)_(W') \( m_i S'_0 \) . $

These are actual line bundles. Under the numerical identification with the base-field twist, their first Chern classes satisfy

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.9)")
 $ frac(c_1 \( cal(Q)_i \), R) = B_p / p + frac(p - 1, 2 p) K_W + lambda_i S_0 = Q_(lambda_i) + frac(B_p - p L - K_W \/ 2, p) \, #h(2em) lambda_i = m_i / R in \[ 0 \, q \] . $ <eq:finite-determinant-classes>]

The numerator of the last term belongs to a fixed finite list. Thus the endpoint comparison bounds the intersection of each normalized class with $gamma$ by $\( 40 epsilon.alt + O \( p^(- 1) \) \) J_x gamma$, uniformly in the chosen columns.

This intersection bound controls sections on the reduction itself. Put $x' = F \( x \)$. For any nonzero section of $cal(Q)_i$, the strict transform of its zero divisor on the blowup at $x'$ is effective and pairs nonnegatively with the twist of $gamma$: that curve is still the pushforward of the fixed ample complete intersection. Since $J_x gamma > 0$, every such section has order at $x'$ at most $R \( 40 epsilon.alt + O \( p^(- 1) \) \)$. Bases adapted to vanishing order in the two factors show that a nonzero section of their external product has order at most the sum of these bounds. Within each bidegree the leading tensors are linearly independent, and different bidegrees cannot cancel. Consequently

$ "ord"_(\( x' \, x' \)) sigma lt.eq R \( 80 epsilon.alt + O \( p^(- 1) \) \) . $

But the rank of $Phi_p$ at $\( x' \, x' \)$ is less than $R \/ 4$. Constant row operations make more than $3 R \/ 4$ rows of the selected matrix vanish at that point, forcing $"ord"_(\( x' \, x' \)) sigma gt.eq 3 R \/ 4$. The two orders contradict $80 epsilon.alt < 3 \/ 4$ for large $p$. The generic-rank calculation used $z_(*)$, whereas this diagonal comparison uses the independently chosen point $x$.

Thus the terminal counterexample in 3 cannot exist. Canonical section spaces are preserved by its birational construction, so this proves 1.

== 8 A section on the original normal variety <sec:conclusion>

The geometric argument produces a pluricanonical section on a smooth fourfold. Hashizume’s reduction gives an effective real representative of an lc adjoint on the original variety. The following elementary lemma turns that representative into a rational one. It concerns finite linear algebra and uses no nonvanishing or abundance theorem.

#thm([Lemma 8.1.])[
  (Finite rational feasibility).  #emph[Let $M = \( m_(i j) \) in "Mat"_(w times v) \( bb(Q) \)$ and $d in bb(Q)^w$. If the system 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.1)")
 $ d_i - y_i = sum_(j = 1)^v m_(i j) b_j quad \( 1 lt.eq i lt.eq w \) \, #h(2em) y_i gt.eq 0 $ <eq:rational-feasibility>]

 has a real solution, it has a rational solution. Moreover, one may require precisely the same $y_i$ to be zero as in the given solution. Consequently, if $D$ is a rational Weil divisor on a normal integral variety $X$ over a field $k$ and $D tilde.op_(bb(R)) G$ for an effective real Weil divisor $G$, then $D tilde.op_(bb(Q)) G_(bb(Q))$ for an effective rational divisor with $"Supp" G_(bb(Q)) = "Supp" G$. If $r D$ is Cartier for a prescribed positive integer $r$, then $H^0 \( X \, cal(O)_X \( m r D \) \) eq.not 0$ for some $m > 0$.]
] <lem:rational-feasibility>

#proof[
  Fix the equations $y_i = 0$ at all coordinates vanishing in the given real solution. Together with the displayed equations they define a nonempty affine space over $bb(Q)$. Gaussian elimination over $bb(Q)$ gives a rational particular solution and a basis over $bb(Q)$ for its direction space. Thus its rational points are dense in its real points. Approximate the given point closely enough to keep all of the finitely many remaining coordinates $y_i$ strictly positive. This proves the first assertion, including the case where all $y_i$ vanish or the affine space has dimension zero.
  For the divisor assertion, choose a finite expression
  $ D - G = sum_(j = 1)^v a_j "Div" \( f_j \) \, #h(2em) a_j in bb(R) \, quad f_j in k \( X \)^(*) . $
  List the prime divisors in the supports of $D \, G$, and these finitely many principal divisors as $P_1 \, dots.h \, P_w$. Write $D = sum d_i P_i$ and let $m_(i j) in bb(Z)$ be the coefficient of $P_i$ in $"Div" \( f_j \)$. The coefficients of $G$ and the $a_j$ give a real solution of (8.1). A rational solution gives $G_(bb(Q)) = sum y_i P_i gt.eq 0$ with the same support and $D - G_(bb(Q)) = sum b_j "Div" \( f_j \)$.
  Choose a positive integer $N$ divisible by $r$ and by the denominators of all $y_i \, b_j$. Then
  $ N D - N G_(bb(Q)) = "Div" #h(-0.16667em) (product_j f_j^(N b_j)) . $
  The effective integral divisor $N G_(bb(Q))$ is Cartier, since $N D$ is Cartier and their difference is principal. It defines a nonzero section of $cal(O)_X \( N D \)$. Write $N = m r$. No $bb(Q)$-factoriality is needed: all equalities were equalities of Weil divisors on $X$. ◻
]

#proof[
  Hashizume’s reduction [20] Theorem 1.4 takes smooth canonical nonvanishing in dimension four, supplied by 1, and gives nonvanishing for projective lc pairs with effective real boundaries and pseudo-effective real Cartier adjoints in dimensions at most four. Applied to the given rational lc pair, it produces $D tilde.op_(bb(R)) G gt.eq 0$ on the original normal variety; nefness implies pseudo-effectivity. 20 gives a nonzero section of $m r D$ for some positive integer $m$, with the prescribed index $r$. ◻
]

